{
  "decision": "revise",
  "headline": "Initiate NVIDIA Q3 FY27 Data Center at $103bn; calendar 2026 $374.62bn versus $372bn street",
  "note": "I initiate coverage with calendar 2026 capex of $682.5bn, $15bn above the October 5 street: Microsoft $150bn cash PP&E, Alphabet $180bn purchases, Amazon $215bn gross purchases, and Meta $137.5bn including finance-lease principal. First-half spending supports modest second-half acceleration; Meta stays at its latest guide midpoint [msft-2026-07-29-release] [googl-2026-07-22-release] [amzn-2026-07-30-release] [meta-2026-07-29-release].\n\nMy unreported NVIDIA Q3 FY27 Data Center estimate is $103bn, expected November 18, 2026; the date is unconfirmed. I assume $110.5bn total revenue, 2.3% above guidance, less $7.5bn Edge. Recent total-revenue beats were larger. The revised annual base is $374.62bn versus $372bn street, requiring $129.86bn Q4; my own Q4 view is $128bn. I narrow the starting tails because their quarterly bridges were implausible [nvda-2026-08-26-release] [nvda-2026-08-26-cfo].\n\nAMD is $14.00bn versus $14bn street: combined EPYC/Instinct disclosure cannot establish a GPU-only variant. Named Helios deployments challenge the delay premise; AWS margin expansion challenges its short premise. Watch late-October capex and November shipment conversion [amd-2026-08-04-release] [amzn-2026-07-30-release] [street-oct2026].\n\nPosition read: NVIDIA/MSFT longs strengthen; AMD/AMZN shorts weaken; Alphabet’s TPU-cost thesis remains untested. NVIDIA’s street gap moves from -$5.62bn to +$2.62bn, AMD from +$1.22bn to $0.00bn; MSFT/GOOGL/AMZN capex gaps each rise $5bn.",
  "stance": "Capex tracks modestly above the street; NVIDIA benefits, while undisclosed GPU revenue keeps AMD’s base near consensus.",
  "rationale": "The first-half capex run rates justify $5bn increases for Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon, with timing and measures explicitly separated. Meta's current guide already supports street. Filing-exact NVIDIA quarters plus the $108bn total Q3 guide constrain plausible calendar-year tails; my $103bn Q3/$128bn Q4 bridge yields $373.37bn, close to the $374.62bn top-down base. AMD's base is re-anchored to consensus because public segment revenue combines CPUs and GPUs. All share and concentration sizes remain forecasting judgments.",
  "thesis": "Calendar 2026 capex is $682.5bn versus $667.5bn synthetic street (+2.25%); NVIDIA Data Center is $374.62bn versus $372bn (+0.70%), and AMD Data Center GPU is $14.00bn versus $14bn. This is modest upside, not proof of a broad street miss. Base mix/NVIDIA split/Big Four concentration remain 38%/65%/45%; AMD split is 2.7%, concentration 50%. Q3 FY27 NVIDIA Data Center is forecast at $103bn before reporting, expected 2026-11-18 unconfirmed; own Q4 is $128bn versus $129.86bn required. Recast Hyperscale is broader than the four customers and cannot establish their revenue share. Custom silicon, deployment timing and undisclosed GPU revenue remain the main risks.",
